  "tips": {
    "greetings": "💡 \"Oi\" and \"Olá\" are both common greetings. Brazilians are warm — expect cheek kisses in social settings.",
    "food": "💡 \"Prato feito\" (PF) plates at lunch offer huge, cheap meals. Say \"sem carne\" if you're vegetarian.",
    "directions": "💡 Uber is reliable and widely used in Brazilian cities. Metered taxis are safe; avoid unmarked cabs.",
    "numbers": "💡 Currency is the Brazilian Real (R$). ATMs are everywhere; Nubank and Itaú are reliable.",
    "emergency": "💡 Dial 190 (police), 192 (ambulance), 193 (fire). Keep photocopies of documents — keep the original in the hotel safe.",
    "shopping": "Watch the card terminal, and prefer a card or PIX to a large wad of cash.",
    "medical": "Call 192 for an ambulance. Travelers usually use a private hospital.",
    "immigration": "Have your passport and the address. Photograph the entry stamp.",
    "hotel": "Ask which streets are fine to walk at night. Use an app car after dark.",
    "transport": "Uber works in the big cities. On a bus, keep your phone in a front pocket."
  },
  "pronGuide": {
    "title": "Brazilian Portuguese Sounds",
    "tips": [
      "Nasal vowels (ã, ão, em, en) — let air flow through your nose, like \"sang\" without the G",
      "The letters LH make a \"ly\" sound (like million) and NH makes \"ny\" (like canyon)",
      "S at end of syllable sounds like \"sh\" in São Paulo dialect (SH-ão POW-loo)",
      "R at start of word or doubled (rr) sounds like the English H"
    ]
  },
